Z||000000||Formula||07-22-99||01:54 PM||||Does anyone know if you can use acryllic enamel paint on a fiero, or do you need a special type of paint?
Z||000001||PaUL||07-22-99||07:17 PM||||I think you could use that paint with a urethane flex additive. When I had my fiero painted a few years ago, I used a paint called Dupont Imron. I never heard of it but the guy that painted my car said it was glossiest paint available. When he was done, I couldnt believe how shiny my car was. It was so shiny, it actually blinded on coming traffic in bright sunlight, and this was the color black.  
Z||000002||Cooter||07-22-99||07:47 PM||||If you dont get the Imron, at least get a base coat/clear coat paint. There are pro's and con's for single stage (enamal) and base/clear but a properly applied bc/cc paint job will look better for a longer period of time with less polishing/waxing than single stage enamal. The bc/cc just doesnt oxidize like the enamal. I used PPG's Omni brand of basecoat and the PPG 2020 high build clear on my fastback SE and it looks WONDERFUL! Dont let the body shop rip you off on materials- you can get the car painted with about 1.5 quarts of unmixed base (which makes 3 quarts of thinned paint) and probably 2 quarts of unmixed clear (it makes 4 quarts when mixed with the reducer and hardner. The gloss of the single stage doesnt compare to the bc/cc finish.
Z||000003||Formula||07-22-99||07:48 PM||||do you know how much that paint is per gallon?  and does it need a clearcoat?
Z||000004||Formula||07-22-99||07:50 PM||||the Imron ^
Z||000005||PaUL||07-22-99||07:52 PM||||I forget how much the Imron paint costs but I remember it was more than the acryllic enamel. <p>I don't like the base coat/clear coat paints. The clear yellows with age and can become cracked and flacky.
Z||000006||PaUL||07-22-99||07:53 PM||||The Imron needs no clear coat.
