Z||000010||Cooter||07-22-99||11:26 PM||||The Imron doesnt need a clear coat, but one is available if you want to spend even more money! Imron was developed for use on airplanes, firetrucks and other large- therefore seldom waxed/polished- vehicles. Most shops in my area wont even talk about spraying Imron anymore. It is really loaded with polyiscynates-aka a derivitive of Cynide the death chamber gas. I dont like being in the same room with it! If you are going with a non-metallic color, you can save some money by not using the bc/cc paint system. But metallic colors in single stage usually are not very slick and buffing them causes the metallics to look streaked. If you are painting a solid (non metallic) color, at the minimum use a polyurethane enamal. Imron is polyurethane also, but other brands are not as expensive. PPG makes a good (economical) polyurethane enamal, as does sikkens, BASF and several others. I dont like Sherwin Williams paint- too much trouble with color matches- they cant even match paint that they have mixed if you need some later for touch up. Last thought- Of all the bc/cc paints I have used in the past 10 years, none have had the clear turn yellow or fade except the Sherwin williams.
Z||000011||lowCG||07-23-99||01:09 AM||||I used to spray airplane paint,the isocyanides aren't any worse than the tolulol,benzine in the normal paints,unless you paint with them everyday,or expose yourself in a way that joepordizes your health,regardless of content.<br>I like the idea of a dent resistant car with Imron,not trying to polish orange peel however! Would want to make real sure the painter was conscientious/familiar with the slight differences in this type of paint compared to,say Delglo,or whatever it is.<br>I believe that the better quality polyurethanes are actually weakened by the use of flex additives,especially in the quantities that are recommended to be used,stay away from that stuff myself,maybe use a teeny bit if there's obvious,constant movement;the surface bond is more critical of a factor.
Z||000012||Formula||07-23-99||09:14 AM||||I was told that if I didn't use a flex additive that the paint would crack up every place.  
Z||000013||Cooter||07-23-99||09:27 AM||||At the Dupont paint school I went to they took a piece of aluminum foil, sprayed it with etching primer and then painted it with their bc/cc sytem. It was then baked to cure the paint. The instructor then took the foil and crumpled it up into a ball, then unrolled the foil, smoothed it out with his hand and there was no paint cracked or flaked from it. Enamal usually needs a flex agent, but I have never used one in the polyurethane or bc/cc paints when I paint bumper covers etc. And an active charcoal paint mask takes care of most of the paint's solvents, but not isocynates. For them a supplied air respirator should be used. But then again if I was that worried about death by paint fumes, I'd pick a safer profession like teaching elementary school. Wait a minute, I think I have that job too. . . .damn- school starts back in how many weeks?
